Patch 06e: orientation partition pruning, block-size-graded evidence.
Supersedes 06, 06b, 06c and 06d -- apply INSTEAD of any of them.

Built from the marginal ratios across the three measured variants rather than
from another hypothesis. For each change, the speed given up divided by the
BD-rate recovered says whether the prunes it removed were worth keeping: a
change raises the ratio only when its marginal ratio is BELOW the ratio you
already have, and the bar is 20.

    A1  variance floor              +2.04% speed for +0.00% BD   marginal  inf
    A1  frame gate                  +5.89% speed for +0.46% BD   marginal 12.8
    A2  var floor + rect floor 32   +8.95% speed for +0.37% BD   marginal 24.2
    A2  frame gate + ext floor 32   +3.36% speed for +0.46% BD   marginal  7.3

Three conclusions, and the third reverses what 06c was built on.

1. The variance floor is deleted. It cost 2.04% of the 4K speedup and returned
   0.00% BD-rate -- not a small return, none at all. Every variant since 06b has
   been carrying it.

2. The frame gate is kept, restored from 06c. At marginal 12.8 the prunes it
   removes are worth well under the bar, so removing them raises the ratio.
   06d deleted it on the argument that a source-based profile is most
   trustworthy on intra frames. The measurement disagreed, taking A1 from 20.9
   to 15.9. The argument was not wrong about where the profile is accurate;
   accuracy was simply not the deciding factor. A prune on a frame the whole
   GOP predicts from is paid for by every frame that follows it, however well
   justified it looked at the time.

3. The resolution-scaled block-size floor is deleted, and replaced by its
   opposite.

   Raising the floor to 32 on 1080p removed prunes worth marginal 24.2 --
   comfortably ABOVE the bar. The 16x16 to 31x31 blocks that 06c excluded were
   the *good* prunes, which is why 06c moved A2 backwards, 14.4 -> 13.5, rather
   than forwards. The damage on 1080p sits in the large blocks, not the small
   ones, and 06c was excluding precisely the wrong end.

   In hindsight this is the reading that should have come first. The profile
   measures orientation about equally well at any block size; what does not
   scale is the cost of being wrong. A mistaken prune on a 128x128 block
   commits sixty-four times the area to a worse partition than the same mistake
   on a 16x16. Large blocks are not less measurable, they are more expensive to
   get wrong -- so they should clear a HIGHER bar, not be exempted from the test
   or excluded from it.

   So the block-size floor stays at 16 everywhere, and the anisotropy required
   scales with block size instead: base for blocks under 32, doubled at 32-63,
   quadrupled at 64 and above. The two multipliers compose, so a large block on
   a heavily referenced frame must clear a very high bar while a small block on
   a leaf frame is pruned on the original evidence.

Expected direction. On A1, dropping the variance floor alone returns 2.04%
speedup at zero BD-rate cost, which on its own would take 06c's 6.07% / 0.29%
to roughly 8.1% / 0.29% -- a ratio near 28. The block-size grading then trades
some of that back. On A2, restoring the 16-31 blocks returns prunes worth 24.2
while the grading removes the large-block prunes the data blames.

Honest odds on A2. It has never exceeded 14.4 in any measured variant, and its
best value came from the original patch with no corrections at all. Predicting
it clears 20 here would be optimistic. What I can say is that this is the first
version aimed at where the A2 measurements actually locate the damage, instead
of at where I assumed it was. If it lands short, the remaining lever I believe
in is the one named two rounds ago and still unbuilt: the profile is measured
on the SOURCE, and A2 spends most of its 33 frames on inter blocks where the
residual, not the source, decides whether a cut pays.

06c remains the measured, known-good fallback for 4K: 06c + 09b gives +11.87%
at ratio 22.0 and is shippable today if this does not beat it.

